C:Stan Rogers, music
C:Jon Berger, lyrics
M:4/4
L:1/8
K:D
P:A
d/2e/2 | fa ag/2f/2 ((3ede) fA | Bddc B3 A | Bd dc/2B/2 Ad d>e |\
|1 ((3fga) gf e3 :|2 ((3fga) fe d3 |] 
P:B
d | bb ba/2g/2 aa a>g| ((3fed) de f3 d | bb ba/2g/2 aa a>g | ((3fed) gf e3 d/2e/2 |
fa ag/2f/2 ((3ede) fA | Bddc B3 A | Bd dc/2B/2 Ad d>e | ((3fga) fe d3 |] 
W:
W: 1. John Barleycorn to the sea’s gone down in a ship both stout and new
W: The thirst to slake of Captain Drake and all his loyal crew
W: To venture brave over wind and wave, the Spaniard for to halt
W: For though he die of Spanish grape, he’ll live as English malt
W: 
W: (Chorus:) 
W: So we’ll strike him down, and we’ll bind him around, and we’ll serve him worse than that
W: We’ll grind his bones between two stones, and bung him in a vat
W: Then we’ll drink his health in a nut brown ale and raise our glasses high
W: For before that he can live again, John Barleycorn must die
W:
W: John Barleycorn to the courting’s gone all dressed in fine array
W: In pewter clad from toe to head, to win a lady gay
W: The poetry that he declaims will stand him in good stead
W: The ladies fair will all declare they love it more than bread
W: 
W: (Chorus)
W: 
W: John Barleycorn’s to the hangman gone, this tale I will unfold
W: For robbing loyal Englishmen of their silver and their gold
W: In a grave unmarked by cross or stone John Barleycorn is lain
W: ‘Til summer’s rains have come and gone and he rises up again
W: 
W: (Chorus)

